Eight members of Turkish LGBTQ+ ⁠rights group ​Kaos GL were jailed pending trial on Tuesday as part of a national operation the government ​says is protecting family values and children, but which rights groups say targets fundamental rights.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a3ee4232-b1d6-11f1-be40-e59e88953996/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-131758-131949-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a3ee4232-b1d6-11f1-be40-e59e88953996/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-131758-131949-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">aa3bcd94-b1d6-11f1-a624-3d6abb56c11d</guid><pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 14:57:43 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Perspective</category><title>Emergency doctor on the challenges of caring for civilians in conflict zones</title><link>http://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20260916-emergency-doctor-on-the-challenges-of-caring-for-civilians-in-conflict-zones</link><description>
For humanitarian doctors and healthcare workers operating in active conflict zones, the challenges are seemingly endless: from so-called "double tap" strikes that put emergency workers at risk, to roadblocks, evacuation orders and even the positioning of humanitarian organisations in the political debate. Pierre Catoire, emergency doctor and vice-president of Mehad — A French NGO dedicated to healthcare in conflict zones — speaks to FRANCE 24 about how medical professionals go about their work in such conditions.
